INT. OCEAN-FLOOR RESEARCH HABITAT - GALLEY - NIGHT

A cylinder of brushed steel and humming pipes. A porthole shows only blackness and the occasional drifting flake of snow. Three tin cups steam on a narrow table.

DR. SERAPHINA OYELARAN-VOSS (50s, marine biologist, silver braid, a mug warming her palms) sits motionless, eyes on the dark glass. Across from her, ENGINEER ILKA MARCHETTI (40s, grease under her nails, a stubborn jaw) stirs her coffee without drinking it. At the end of the table, DR. KOFI LUNDGREN (30s, hydrologist) taps a stylus against a dead tablet.

The hull groans. A distant, long, patient sound, like a whale in a tunnel. No one flinches. They have heard it for weeks.

SERAPHINA
Fourteen days of nothing.

KOFI
Fourteen days of the same nothing.

ILKA
The pumps are fine. The scrubbers are fine. The only thing that isn't fine is the schedule.

SERAPHINA
She wants us topside by Thursday.

ILKA
She wants a lot of things.

KOFI
Who is "she"?

ILKA
Command. Surface. The woman with the clipboard.

SERAPHINA
She means well.

ILKA
She means to bring us up before we have finished, and she knows it.

Kofi sets down his stylus. Looks at the porthole, then at the two women, then at the cups.

KOFI
Did she say why Thursday?

SERAPHINA
The storm window closes.

ILKA
Storm window. Funding window. Pick one.

The hull groans again, lower. Seraphina rests her hand on the table and feels it hum through the steel.

SERAPHINA
Then we use the days we have.

ILKA
She will not like that.

SERAPHINA
She does not have to be here.

Ilka almost smiles. At last she drinks.
